    "body": "Minister Julio Moltó (left) talks to Cobre Panama mine employees. ( Image courtesy of Ministry of Commerce and Industries. )\nPosted By: Cecilia Jamasmie November 17, 2026\nPanama has authorized formal negotiations with First Quantum Minerals (TSX: FM) over a potential restart of mining at Cobre Panamá, advancing efforts to resolve a dispute that halted extraction at the copper operation three years ago.\nAn interministerial team led by Commerce and Industries Minister Julio Moltó will negotiate with the Canadian miner’s local subsidiary, Minera Panamá. Discussions will cover employment, economic benefits, environmental oversight and the termination of arbitration proceedings against the country, President José Raúl Mulino announced Monday.\n“We are unclear when and if any of Panama’s negotiating position (fiscal/legal frameworks) will be released but see the commencement of negotiations as a key and positive step to enable a restart of mining operations before broken rock stockpiles run out,” BMO mining analyst Matthew Murphy wrote in a note Tuesday.\nThe talks could restore extraction at an operation that produced about 331,000 tonnes of copper in 2023. Cobre Panamá is already processing previously mined material under a separate authorization, but those stockpiles are expected to last only until mid-2027. A new agreement would be needed for a broader restart.\nTerms of return\nFirst Quantum shares rose. 7.4% to $32.093 on Tuesday morning in Toronto after they closed 7.6% weaker at $29.81 on Monday before Mulino’s announcement, giving it a market value of about $26.7 billion. The stock has traded between $27.68 and $49.27 over the past year. BMO rates the shares outperform with a $60 target.\nMulino outlined five principles governing negotiations: Panamanian ownership of mineral resources, maximum economic benefits for the country, national sovereignty, strict environmental protection and compliance with the constitution and national laws.\nAny agreement must include an orderly, verifiable and definitive closure plan financed by the company, with reforestation and measures to prevent environmental damage. Closure would be mandatory, with no possibility of an extension, the presidency said.\nMulino said an agreement would not constitute a mining concession. The government would determine the legal structure for any deal, while public participation, including consultation with nearby communities, would accompany negotiations. No restart date or operating term has been announced.\nFirst Quantum welcomed the announcement Tuesday and said public participation would take place alongside the talks and before any agreement is reached. A restart could create 6,000 direct and 30,000 indirect jobs and business opportunities for about 2,000 registered suppliers, it estimated.\nStockpiles already processing\nMining has been suspended at the site, about 120 km west of Panama City, since November 2023, when Panama’s Supreme Court ruled its operating contract unconstitutional following nationwide protests.\nStockpile processing began in the second quarter of this year. First Quantum expects it to yield 30,000–40,000 tonnes of copper in 2026, with the remaining material processed into next year, the company said in October.\nSGS delivered its government-commissioned audit on June 19. The review produced a weighted overall compliance score of 88%, while identifying shortcomings in flora protection, ecological restoration and reforestation, Panama’s government said. The findings did not authorize a restart.\nA government commission recommended negotiations on Sept. 30 to assess a new agreement that would settle arbitration claims and allow operations to finance an orderly closure without cost to the state. Most of Monday’s conditions had already been disclosed, Murphy said.\n“Most of the requirements stated by the President have been named in previous disclosures,” Murphy wrote. “The statement that the mine life cannot be extended was included in the high-level commission report.”\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: Canada Mexico and Central America\nCOMPANY: First Quantum Minerals\nRepublish this article",
